course: religion-ethics
lesson: 1
title: Creation: God the maker
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The Bible begins with Genesis 1, where God makes the world in six days: light, sky, sea and land, plants, the sun, moon and stars, fish and birds, animals and, last of all, people.
explain: After each day's work the story repeats "God saw that it was good", and on the seventh day God rests and blesses the day, which is where the weekly day of rest comes from.
explain: Christians believe this means the world is not an accident: it was made on purpose and with care, and every person, made "in God's image", has special worth.
explain: God gives people the job of looking after the earth, like caretakers in a borrowed house, not owners who may use everything up.
explain: Christians disagree about how to read the six days, as literal days or as a poem-like story, but they agree on the main message: God is the creator, and science and faith can ask different questions.
explain: For a child today that means treating the world as a gift (no littering, kindness to animals), resting well, and respecting every person, even someone you do not like.
fact: Genesis 1 tells how God made light, sky, sea, land, plants, sun, moon and stars, fish, birds, animals and people over six days.
fact: After each day of making, Genesis says that God saw that what he had made was good.
fact: Christians believe the world was made on purpose and with care, and that it is a good gift to enjoy and look after.
fact: Genesis 1 says God made man and woman in his own image, so Christians believe every person has special worth and deserves respect.
fact: People are made last, so many Christians see them as the high point of creation, made to care for the rest.
fact: On the seventh day God rested and blessed the day; this is the root of the weekly day of rest, the Sabbath.
fact: God gives people the job of caring for the animals and the land (Genesis 1 and 2); this is called stewardship.
fact: A steward is a caretaker who looks after something that belongs to someone else.
fact: Genesis 2 tells of the Garden of Eden, a beautiful garden where God put the first people to live and to work.
fact: Some Christians read the six days as six literal days; many read them as a poem-like story that teaches who made the world and why (disputed among Christians).
fact: Many Christians accept the Big Bang and evolution and see God behind them; some Christians do not (disputed).
fact: A common way to put it: science asks how the world works, faith asks why it exists and what it is for.
fact: Psalm 8 looks up at the moon and stars and wonders why God cares about small human beings.
fact: Feeling small under the night sky can lead to wonder and thankfulness rather than fear.
exercise: In the story of Genesis 1, over how many days does God make the world? | Six days, then he rests on the seventh | Seven days, working hard on every single one | Forty days and forty nights, like the great flood | One day, all of it at once early in the morning
exercise: What did God make last of all in Genesis 1? | People, a man and a woman | The sun, moon and stars | Fish and the birds of the air | Light, to end the darkness
exercise: What does Genesis keep saying after each day of making? | God saw that it was good | God saw that it was not finished | God was tired and needed sleep | God asked the angels what they thought
exercise: Why do you think the story keeps repeating that creation was good? | To show the world is a good gift, worth enjoying and looking after | To show that God was not sure and kept checking | Because the writer forgot he had already said it | To show that only the sea and sky were good
exercise: What does it mean that people are made "in God's image"? | Every person has special worth and deserves respect | People look exactly like God does from the outside, in every way | Only kings and queens are like God | People are gods themselves and need no help
exercise: If everyone is made in God's image, how should we treat someone we do not like? | With respect, because they have worth too | We may ignore them, since we do not like them | We should treat them well only if they are nice first | We should copy whatever they do to us
exercise: What did God do on the seventh day? | He rested and blessed the day | He made the animals of the land | He made the first garden | He started the whole world again
exercise: Why might a day of rest be good for families and for the world? | People are more than their work, and rest gives time for God, family and recovery | Because work on that day is always against the law of every country | Because resting means you never have to work again | Because only lazy people need rest, and they need a day too
exercise: What job does God give people in Genesis 1 and 2? | To care for the animals and the land | To own the earth and use up whatever they like | To stay away from animals and plants | To build cities over all the gardens
exercise: A steward or caretaker of the earth is someone who... | looks after it for God, not as its owner | can sell any part of it they like for money | only cares about the bits of it that they like | lets other people look after it for them instead
exercise: What was the Garden of Eden? | A beautiful garden where God put the first people to live and work | The garden of a king in Egypt | A garden where people were not allowed to work at all, only to sleep and eat | A park that Noah planted after the flood
exercise: What does the story of the Garden of Eden teach about the world? | God wants people to enjoy it and take care of it | God wants people to stay indoors and away from nature | The world is only for animals, not for people | Gardens are more holy than cities and homes
exercise: How do Christians read the six days of Genesis 1? | Some as literal days, many as a poem-like story about who made the world and why | All Christians read them as exactly 24-hour days, as the only true way to read them | All Christians say the story is not about God at all | Christians are not allowed to read Genesis 1
exercise: What do all Christians agree on about Genesis 1? | God is the creator of the world | The world is exactly six thousand years old | Science is always wrong about the stars | The world made itself without a purpose
exercise: What do many Christians think about the Big Bang and evolution? | Many accept them and see God behind them, though some do not | Every Christian rejects them completely | Every Christian thinks they prove there is no God, so they reject them | Christians have never heard of them
exercise: Which question belongs to science and which to faith? | Science asks how the world works; faith asks why it exists | Science asks why the world exists; faith asks how it works | Both ask only how many stars there are | Neither asks any questions about the world
exercise: What does Psalm 8 wonder about when it looks at the stars? | Why God cares about small human beings | How far away the moon is in miles | Why the stars are not as good as people | Who will count all the stars first
exercise: How can feeling small under the night sky help a person? | It can lead to wonder and thankfulness | It should make them feel worthless | It means they should stop looking at the sky | It proves people do not matter to anyone
test: my little bro says God made people first and then all the animals. right? | No, in Genesis 1 people come last, after the animals | Yes, people were made on the very first day, before the light | Yes, and the plants came on the seventh day | No, the animals made the people
test: Zara, who is Muslim, asks: "Do Christians think the world just happened by chance?" What is the best answer? | No, Christians believe it was made on purpose and with care by God | Yes, Christians think there was no plan at all, only chance and luck | Christians say nobody can ask that question | Christians think the world was made by people
test: Tom, who doesn't believe in God, says: "Science explains the Big Bang, so your story's pointless." What could a Christian reply? | Many Christians accept the Big Bang; Genesis answers why there is a world, not how | Christians must say the Big Bang is a lie | Christians agree Genesis is pointless now | Science and faith can never talk about the world at all
test: lol why'd God need a rest, was he knackered or what | The story teaches that rest is good and holy, not that God was tired | Yes, God was worn out after so much work | He rested because he ran out of ideas | He rested because the people he had just made told him to stop working
test: Mia drops her crisp packet in the park: "Who cares, it's not my park." What does stewardship say? | We are caretakers of the world, so we look after it even if we do not own it | She is right, only the owner should care | Only gardeners have to look after parks | Rubbish is fine because the earth is very big
test: kid in my class is proper annoying, does "God's image" stuff even count for him?? | Yes, every person has special worth, even people we find annoying | No, only kind people are made in God's image, not the annoying ones | Only if he goes to church | Only grown-ups are made in God's image
test: Your cousin says: "All Christians believe the world was made in six 24-hour days." What is more accurate? | Some do, but many read the days as a poem-like story; Christians disagree | Yes, every Christian believes exactly that | No Christian has ever believed that | Christians say the days were six years long
test: Mum says "Sunday's family day, phones off." Your sister moans "that's just a made-up rule". Where does the idea come from? | From the seventh day in Genesis, when God rested and blessed the day | From a law made by phone companies | From the story of the Garden of Eden only, where nobody had any phones | From a rule that children must never play
test: Dad on the camping trip, looking at the stars: "Makes you feel tiny, doesn't it?" Which Bible poem says the same? | Psalm 8, which wonders why God cares about small human beings | Genesis 1, the list of six days | The story of the Garden of Eden | The Ten Commandments
test: Olympiad judge: "If the world belongs to God, may we hunt a whole kind of animal until it is gone?" | No, stewards must look after creation, not use it up | Yes, people own the whole world and can do whatever they like | Yes, as long as it is done on a weekday and not on Sunday | No, because people may never use any animals at all, not even for food
test: Judge's follow-up: "Why does the story say people were made last?" Which answer fits the story best? | People are the high point of creation, made to care for the rest | People were an afterthought that God almost forgot to make at all | God had run out of room for people before | People were made last because they matter least
exam: Identify the principal teaching which Christians derive from the account of creation contained in the first chapter of Genesis. | That the world was brought into being intentionally by God | That the world came into being by chance alone | That the world shall endure for six days only | That human beings were the first of all creatures to be made
exam: Select the response most consistent with the doctrine that humanity is made in the image of God. | Every human person possesses an inherent worth meriting respect | Only persons of high rank possess any worth | Human beings resemble God in outward appearance | Worth is conferred solely upon those persons who conduct themselves well
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the position of Christians in respect of the six days of creation? | It is disputed, some reading them literally and many figuratively | It is uniformly held that they were literal days | It is uniformly held that the account is without meaning | The matter has never been the subject of discussion
exam: Ascertain the meaning of "stewardship" as it pertains to the natural world in Christian teaching. | The responsible care of creation on behalf of God | The unrestricted ownership and use of all resources | The abstention from all contact with the natural world | The exclusive care of gardens by ordained ministers
